ANSIBLE INSTALLATION & EXECUTION GUIDE 

1.  Install Ansible on the Control Node (Ubuntu VM)
    -   Ansible should be installed only on the machine that will manage
        other servers.
2.  Do NOT install Ansible on EC2 target machines
    -   Managed nodes only require SSH and Python, not Ansible
        installation.
3.  sudo apt update
    -   Updates the package list to ensure latest repository
        information.
4.  sudo apt install ansible -y
    -   Installs Ansible and all required dependencies.
5.  ansible –version
    -   Verifies Ansible installation and displays installed version.
6.  chmod 400 ubuntu-key.pem
    -   Sets correct permissions for SSH private key.
7.  ssh -i ubuntu-key.pem ubuntu@
    -   Tests manual SSH login to target EC2 instance.
8.  mkdir ansible-project
    -   Creates a dedicated directory for Ansible project files.
9.  cd ansible-project
    -   Moves into the Ansible project directory.
10. nano inventory.ini
    -   Creates the inventory file to define managed servers.
11. Add EC2 details inside inventory.ini
    -   Specifies host IP, SSH user, and private key path for remote
        access.
12. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -m ping
    -   Tests connectivity between control node and managed EC2
        instance.
13. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “uptime”
    -   Executes a simple remote command to confirm remote execution
        works.
14. nano apache-playbook.yml
    -   Creates an Ansible playbook file.
15. ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini apache-playbook.yml
    -   Executes the playbook on all defined slave servers.
16. ssh -i ubuntu-key.pem ubuntu@13.218.95.205
    -   Connects securely to the EC2 instance using the private key.
17. exit
    -   Exits from the EC2 instance and returns to the control node
        terminal.
18. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -m ping
    -   Verifies communication between control node and managed EC2
        server.
19. ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini apache-playbook.yml
    -   Executes the Apache installation playbook on target servers.
20. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo ss -tulpn”
    -   Displays all listening ports and associated services on the
        target server.
21. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo lsof -iTCP:80 -sTCP:LISTEN”
    -   Identifies which service is occupying port 80.
22. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo systemctl stop nginx”
    -   Stops nginx service to free port 80.
23. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo systemctl disable nginx”
    -   Prevents nginx from starting automatically on reboot.
24. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo systemctl start apache2”
    -   Starts Apache service after resolving port conflict.
25. ansible -i inventory.ini slaves -a “sudo systemctl status apache2”
    -   Verifies that Apache is active and running successfully.
26. Open http://13.218.95.205 in browser
    -   Confirms Apache web server is serving the deployed web page.
